    "result_text": "The full build succeeds with all tests green. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the email-uniqueness rule for owner creation: rejecting a new owner whose lower-cased email already belongs to any other owner, responding with **409 Conflict**.\n\nFollowing the existing patterns in the codebase (mirroring how duplicate telephone and duplicate household are handled), I added:\n\n1. **`DuplicateEmailException`** (`rest/controller/`) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` signaling the conflict, parallel to `DuplicateTelephoneException`.\n\n2. **`EmailUniquenessValidator`** (`rest/controller/`) \u2014 a small single-responsibility `@Component` that canonicalizes the candidate's email via the existing `EmailNormalizer` (trim + lower-case) and throws `DuplicateEmailException` if any existing owner shares it. Owners without an email (null/blank \u2192 `null`) are never in conflict, matching the optional-email semantics already encoded in `EmailNormalizer`.\n\n3. **Wiring in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injected the validator and called `emailUniquenessValidator.validate(owner)` just before persisting, alongside the other create-time guards.\n\n4. **Exception handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 a `@ExceptionHandler(DuplicateEmailException.class)` returning `409 Conflict` as a `ProblemDetail`, consistent with the sibling duplicate handlers.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` passes, and the full suite builds green. I reused `EmailNormalizer` and `ClinicService.findAllOwners()` rather than duplicating normalization or adding a redundant finder, keeping each piece focused.",
